1 OCCU Deutscher Humanist, Nürnberger Ratsherr, Anwalt und römisch-katholischer Franziskaner und Priester., Lawyer, Jurist, 1478-88 Rat von Herzog Albrecht IV, Gelehrter, Büchersammler
1 TITL Dr. utr. iuris.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://mif.stparchive.com/page_image.php?paper=MIF&year=2004&month=7&day=29&page=4&mode=F&base=MIF07292004P04&title=Mifflinburg%20Telegraph
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Johannes Pirckheimer''' (* um 1440; † 3. Mai 1501) war ein deutscher Humanist, Nürnberger Ratsherr und römisch-katholischer Franziskaner und Priester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pirckheimer wurde als Sohn von Hans Pirckheimer geboren und stammte aus Nürnberg. 1463 wurde er Consilarius der deutschen Nation zu Padua. 1465 wurde er Dr. utr. iuris. 1466 wurde er Sekretär des Bischofs von Eichstätt, Marquard I
2 CONC I. Schenk von Castell. 1467 wurde er Konsulent der Stadt Nürnberg und 1478 Rat von Herzog Albrecht IV. von Bayern, später auch von Herzog Siegmund von Tirol. Nach dem Tod seiner Frau verbrachte er seine letzten Lebensjahre im fran
2 CONC ziskanischen Barfüßerkloster und wurde vier Jahre vor seinem Tod im Jahre 1497 zum Priester geweiht.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quelle: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Pirckheimer
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/pirckheimer-family
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans son Johannes (ca. 1440–1501) served the bishop of Eichstätt and was a founder of the School of Poets in Nürnberg in 1496. He also possessed one of the most important private libraries in Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes's eldest daughter Barbara (1467–1532) entered Nürnberg's convent of Santa Klara in 1479 and changed her name to Caritas. Endowed with an amazing talent for Latin, she helped write the Latin version of the convent's histor
2 CONC y and its German translation. In 1503 Caritas was elected abbess* of the convent. Humanists cited her as the ideal of a learned woman. When the Nürnberg Council adopted Protestantism as the official religion, she came out in oppos
2 CONC ition. She kept the convent open with the aid of her brother Willibald (1470–1530) and the scholar Philipp Melanchthon. However, city authorities would not allow the nuns to receive any sacraments* of the Roman Catholic Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://mif.stparchive.com/Archive/MIF/MIF07292004P04.php
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willibald_Pirckheimer
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.encyclopedia.com/places/germany-scandinavia-and-central-europe/german-political-geography/nuremberg
2 CONT
2 CONT https://gw.geneanet.org/drmarkusvogel?lang=en&pz=markus+werner&nz=vogel&p=johannes&n=pirckheimer
2 CONT
2 CONT http://mif.stparchive.com/page_image.php?paper=MIF&year=2004&month=7&day=29&page=4&mode=F&base=MIF07292004P04&title=Mifflinburg%20Telegraph
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Son Biography states father was a lawyer 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/pirckheimer-family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willibald_Pirckheimer
2 CONT
2 CONT https://peoplepill.com/people/johannes-pirckheimer/
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes Pirckheimer (* around 1440; † May 3, 1501) was a German humanist, Nuremberg councilor and Roman Catholic Franciscan and priest.
2 CONT
2 CONT LIFE Pirckheimer was born the son of Hans Pirckheimer and came from Nuremberg. In 1463 he became consilarius of the German nation in Padua. In 1465 he became Dr. utr. iuris. In 1466 he became secretary of the Bishop of Eichstätt, 
2 CONC Marquard II Schenk von Castell. In 1467 he became a consultant to the city of Nuremberg and in 1478 advice to Duke Albrecht IV of Bavaria, and later to Duke Siegmund of Tyrol. After the death of his wife he spent the last years of
2 CONC  his life in the Franciscan barefoot monastery and was ordained a priest four years before his death in 1497.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN Johannes Pirckheimer had 12 children together with his wife Barbara, néeöffelholz. Of these, one son and seven daughters reached adulthood. Six daughters entered the Poor Clare or Benedictine convent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caritas Pirckheimer (born Barbara Pirckheimer), abbess of the Klarakloster in Nuremberg (1467–1532) Willibald Pirckheimer (1470–1530), humanist, councilor Clara Pirckheimer, Abbess of the Klarakloster in Nuremberg (1480–1533) Sabi
2 CONC na Pirckheimer (1481–1529) Euphemia Pirckheimer (1486–1547) Juliana Pirckheimer, married Geuderin
2 CONT
