1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Page 215 and Tabula VIIII in Gatterer, Johann Christoph: Historia Genealogica Dominorum Holzschuherorum Ab Aspach Et Harlach In Thalheim Cet. Norimbergae: Fleischmannus, 1755
2 CONT
2 CONT "GEORGE VI HOLZSCHUHER, the eldest son of George V, began his life on January 14, 1629. Capable of higher learning, in 1646 he headed to Altdorf, a place renowned for nourishing good minds. There, after having successfully devoted
2 CONC  four years to his studies; on April 4, 1650, he traveled through Swabia, Helvetia, Geneva, and Lyon and entered Paris, where he eagerly and diligently observed everything he deemed beneficial to his purposes. From there, he journ
2 CONC eyed through Blois, Tours, and Saumur, reaching Marseille and the port of Toulon, from where he embarked on a ship and sailed with a favorable wind to Italy. Upon disembarking, he proceeded to Nice. As he made his way to Rome, he 
2 CONC passed through the Principality of Monaco, Genoa, and the Florentine region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leaving Rome, he visited Naples, Ancona, Bologna, Milan, and Venice. He stayed for some time in Padua and took on the role of Procurator of the German Nation. From Italy, he traveled through Trent, Innsbruck, Augsburg, Munich, and
2 CONC  Ingolstadt, and safely returned to his homeland on September 8, 1652. In Nuremberg in 1656, he was appointed as an Assessor to the city's chief tribunal, in 1658 he joined the order of Junior Consuls, and in 1664 was put in charg
2 CONC e of examining and understanding the cases of craftsmen. Furthermore, in 1667 he was attached to the Court of Appeals, in 1670 he presided over military affairs, and in the same year, on November 18, he was made Senior Consul. Fin
2 CONC ally, in 1677 he advanced to the dignity of Septemvir and was appointed as the supreme head of the armory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because he was renowned for his outstanding examples of prudence, and not only was he subtle in judgment but also eloquent in explanation, he was often called upon to resolve difficult matters and undertook serious missions by the
2 CONC  authority of the illustrious Senate. During the time he lived, as many men of the highest dignity came to Nuremberg; no one was considered more suitable or worthy than our man to receive them properly in the name of the Senate an
2 CONC d to demonstrate proper respect to them. Therefore, it cannot be expressed how much all good people mourned his death with groans and lamentation. He passed away on March 24, 1679."
