1 OCCU German Renaissance artist, known for his woodcuts and paintings, Kunstmaler, Painter and Mayor, Maler und Bürgermeister zu Wittenberg
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas_Cranach_the_Younger '''Lucas Cranach the Younger'''] (October 4, 1515 – January 25, 1586) was a German Renaissance artist, known for his woodcuts and paintings.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the youngest son of Lucas Cranach the Elder and Barbara Brengebier, and began his career as an apprentice in his father's workshop alongside his brother Hans. Henceforth, his own reputation and fame grew. After his father's
2 CONC  death, he assumed control over the workshop.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 20, 1541, he married Barbara Brück, who went on to bear three sons and a daughter. She died February 10, 1550, and thereafter Cranach remarried, wedding Magdalena Schurff on May 24, 1551. This union produced three daug
2 CONC hters and two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is known for portraits and simple versions of allegorical and mythical scenes. The style of his paintings can be so similar to those of his father that there have been some difficulties in attribution of their works.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Lucas Cranach the Younger (born October 4, 1515 in Wittenberg , † January 25, 1586 ibid) was an important painter and portraitist of the Renaissance . He is the son of Lucas Cranach the Elder . [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage to Barbara Brück  On February 20, 1541 he married his first wife Barbara Brück (* around 1518 in Wittenberg, † February 10, 1550 ibid), a daughter of the Saxon Chancellor Gregor Brück . Four children emerged from th
2 CONC is connection:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucas (* 1541, † February 16, 1612 in Meißen), he had enrolled at the University of Wittenberg in 1554, was a councilor in Torgau and from January 30, 1591 to January 9, 1609 administrator of the Prince's School St. Afra in Meißen
2 CONC . He married Anna on October 17, 1570 in Wittenberg, the daughter of the secretary Hieronymus Gareis.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Daughter Euphrosyna (* 1585 in Torgau, † August 6, 1627 in Wittenberg) married 1604 with the electoral clerk 
2 CONT      Abel Volk (* July 26, 1575, † January 19, 1622 in Wittenberg, the son of Michael Volk from Dresden and his 
2 CONT      wife Esther née Sierbürger from Leipzig)
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara (* unknown; † 1601), married the medical doctor Johann Hermann on November 28, 1564
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes (* unknown; † May 1, 1548), died in childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian (* unknown; † April 7, 1556), died as a student (immatr. 1554 UWB)
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage to Magdalena Schurff  After the death of Barbara, he married Magdalena Schurff (born August 19, 1531 in Wittenberg, † January 3, 1606 ibid) on May 24, 1551, a daughter of the Saxon personal physician Prof. Dr. Augu
2 CONC stin Schurff and niece of Philipp Melanchthon . They had five children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT Magdalena (* unknown; † 1554), died in childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustin (* 1554, † July 26, 1595), followed his father as a painter, was also a council member, city judge and treasurer in Wittenberg. Married Maria Selfisch, daughter of Samuel Selfisch, on October 26, 1577
2 CONT
2 CONT Agneta (* unknown; † 1560), died in childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Christoph (* around 1557; † February 23, 1596) was a councilor and married Subphrona on February 5, 1583 (Veronika? * September 14, 1563; † April 29, 1629) Vogel
2 CONT
2 CONT     Lucas (born November 30, 1583, † unknown)
2 CONT     Christoph (born January 14, 1585, † unknown)
2 CONT     Barbara (born June 16, 1586, † unknown)
2 CONT     Ernst August (born January 22, 1588, † unknown)
2 CONT     Magdalene (born January 22, 1588, † unknown)
2 CONT     Christian (born October 10, 1590, † unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (born December 3, 1561 in Wittenberg, † September 16, 1645 in Wittenberg); married to Polykarp Leyser the Elder
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas_Cranach_der_J%C3%BCngere
